WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.338
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.679 --> 00:00:08.220
JASON POLITES: This
session is called

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.860
Life of a Serverless Event.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.070
Really, the intent here is
to, just a little bit, lift

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.940
the cover on what
goes on underneath

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:20.530
in the serverless infrastructure
that we have in Cloud,

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.350
the building blocks
that we've built on

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:28.770
to provide the serverless
products that you can use.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:31.340
So before we get started,
I just wanted to level

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.475
set on an assumption here.

00:00:33.475 --> 00:00:34.850
And the assumption
is that we all

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:38.330
agree, in principle, that
serverless generally is

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:39.930
the place that we want to be.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.991
You're not managing
infrastructure.

00:00:41.991 --> 00:00:43.490
You're not dealing
with load spikes,

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.430
we'll deal with that for you.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.140
You're not spending
time managing things

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:50.060
that don't add value
to your business.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:53.840
You've elevated yourself to
a higher plane of existence.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.870
However, there
are some problems.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.886
Definitionally, we gave you
a black box and we said,

00:01:02.886 --> 00:01:04.760
you don't have to worry
about all this magic.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.230
We'll do it for you.

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.660
But that leads to
some issues in terms

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.640
of visibility and transparency.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.040
You might see some
latency increase.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.130
And how do you reason
about what that might be?

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:19.080
Maybe your build time
suddenly got longer.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.590
Why is that?

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:24.050
And then you might
see different behavior

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.120
in one environment
versus another.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.129
Or you're hitting some
weird quota limit.

00:01:28.129 --> 00:01:29.420
And how do you reason about it?

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.000
So the theory is great.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.890
You don't have to
worry about anything.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:36.870
The reality is, you still have
to worry about these things.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.420
So the purpose of
this session is

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.760
to just give you
insight into what's

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:46.730
going on underneath there so
that you can reason about these

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.380
with more intelligence.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.425
And obviously, you would
have logging and monitoring

00:01:51.425 --> 00:01:52.800
and all sorts of
things in place.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.180
But as engineers, I think
it's useful to know,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.980
what are the mechanics
going on under here?

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:02.980
So we'll cover
these four topics.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.190
First of all, what's
the definition

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.610
of serverless compute
on Cloud Platform?

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.380
Secondly, what happens
when you deploy?

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:11.910
What steps do we actually take?

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:17.220
When you write that command
line, G Cloud functions deploy.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.880
What happens when we receive
an event or a request?

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.460
How does that travel through
the system, and what happens?

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.810
And then we'll close
with some best practices.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:27.930
What do you do with
this information

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.220
once we've revealed it to you?

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.550
Serverless compute
on Cloud Platform

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.900
covers two products, mainly,
Cloud Functions and App Engine.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.130
I'll be referring to
these throughout the talk

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.070
as an app and a function.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.010
You deploy an app
with App Engine,

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.720
you deploy a function
with Cloud Functions.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.420
And there's just a distinction
that I've made there.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.390
An app is something
that reacts to requests.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:58.170
A function is something
that reacts to events.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.460
You know, there's many
distinctions you can make.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:04.380
That's just illustrative.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.010
OK, how do we build a
serverless compute platform?

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:13.860
Now, if you wanted to go and
build your own serverless

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.920
compute platform, I'm going
to tell you how to do it now.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:17.870
So take notes.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.710
And if you want to go out
and start building your own,

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.890
then this is how you do it.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.809
Step one, you need a global
footprint of data centers.

00:03:26.809 --> 00:03:27.600
It's pretty simple.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.450
There are actually servers
underneath serverless.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:39.400
As you would know, Google
has a global distribution

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.600
of machines and data centers,
so it all starts there.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.300
The next layer that
we have is something

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.390
that we've talk publicly about.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:49.990
It's this thing called Borg.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:53.110
And what it is is a shared
compute infrastructure

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:55.960
that runs pretty much
every service in Google.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:00.430
Everything from Google
Maps to YouTube to search,

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.950
and also Cloud.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:08.230
And its premise is that
Google engineers, in fact,

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.810
operate serverlessly
and have done

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.370
for a very, very long time.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:14.890
They don't reason about
physical infrastructure.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:19.360
They just create tasks in Borg,
and those tasks go and execute.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.930
The caveat is that it
only runs trusted code.

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:26.650
It runs code that
is cryptographically

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:28.690
verifiable as Google code.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:33.200
And your code, I hate
to say it, is untrusted.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.400
So how do we run untrusted code?

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.980
Earlier in the year
at Cube-Con, we

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:44.650
talked about the open-sourcing
of a project we call gVisor.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:47.860
And gVisor is a
unique technology

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:51.110
that we've created that is
a sandboxing technology.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.830
It allows us to
isolate processes

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.380
on a shared compute
infrastructure from a security

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:57.400
standpoint.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.520
So Process A has no
mechanism by which

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.970
it can interfere with
Process B. This allows

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.430
us to run untrusted
code on an otherwise

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:10.280
trusted infrastructure.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:14.930
So above the data center,
above Borg, above these secured

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:19.160
processes we have a serving,
routing, and scheduling layer.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:24.050
The serving is the data
plane that receives requests.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:25.550
It's responsible for routing.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.790
It does things like, if
a zone in a data center

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:30.710
is unavailable, it
will automatically

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.140
move an application
to a zone that

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:36.220
is available for that sort
of failover guarantee.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:37.970
And the scheduling
part of it is the thing

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.816
that decides whether a new
instance of your application

00:05:40.816 --> 00:05:43.190
needs to be created, whether
there's an old instance that

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:48.830
can be evicted, and makes
those decisions to auto scale

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:51.910
you up and down.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:55.150
And above all of that
is the Google front end.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.070
The Google front
ends sit at the edge.

00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:00.250
They sit in front of every
single Google service.

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.410
Once the request hits
the Google front end,

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.860
from that moment onwards,
it's on the Google network.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.390
It's on the Google operated
network from the Google front

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:07.900
end.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.060
And it has some other
characteristics.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.010
Like it does SSL termination.

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.220
It does some amount of
denial of service protection.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.650
And that's the main
entry point at the edge.

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:25.500
OK, so all of these
layers, does it all work?

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:31.544
If we send this many requests
per year, 127 trillion,

00:06:31.544 --> 00:06:32.710
that's a meaningless number.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:36.340
But really, it's
about four million QPS

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:38.530
on this serverless
infrastructure

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:41.125
across both Cloud
Functions and App Engine.

00:06:41.125 --> 00:06:42.250
That's a pretty big number.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.100
OK, let's get to the good stuff.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.890
I'm going to talk
about the life cycle.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:50.330
What happens when you deploy?

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.760
When you execute
that CLI command,

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.110
what's actually happening?

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:57.440
And then once your application
is serving traffic, how do we

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:58.507
route the request to you?

00:06:58.507 --> 00:06:59.340
And what's going on?

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:00.631
How are we scaling up and down?

00:07:00.631 --> 00:07:02.510
And what are some
of the side effects

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:06.640
of the implementation we have?

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.190
OK, first of all, deployment.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:12.825
You enter this command into
your CLI, or you use the UI.

00:07:12.825 --> 00:07:14.575
And the first thing
that happens is build.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:21.730
Concretely, in the case of App
Engine and Cloud Functions,

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.890
we're going to build
a container image.

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:27.820
And the latency there is going
to be a function primarily

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:30.640
of the file size and
your network speed

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.910
to get the data up to us.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.920
Then we can actually crunch
that together into an image.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:40.780
And most of the time spent
there is resolving dependencies.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:43.180
Particularly in
Cloud Functions, we

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:46.257
have this ability to resolve
the dependencies for you.

00:07:46.257 --> 00:07:48.340
If there's any Node.js
developers in the audience,

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:51.610
you'll recognize this
as a package.json file

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.340
that you will upload along
with your source code that

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:58.090
declares a set of dependencies
in Python, requirements.txt.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:01.670
We'll go and resolve those for
you and bake it into an image

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:06.990
so you don't need to upload all
of your dependencies in one go.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:10.170
Once it's built, we create
or update that instance

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:11.630
on the infrastructure.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:15.402
And the latency there is
a function of two things.

00:08:15.402 --> 00:08:16.860
One is cold starts,
which I'm going

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:18.970
to talk a lot about today.

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:20.880
And the other is
a rolling deploy,

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.560
which doesn't seem like a
latency impacting thing.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.430
But the idea here
is that you already

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.380
have a version of your
application running.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:30.270
You want to make a change to it.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:31.980
That version is already
serving traffic.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:33.480
You don't want to
kill that traffic.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.440
You want to gradually
roll that out.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:38.159
So the time between when
you made the change, when

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:40.919
you updated the function,
and it becoming available

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:42.360
across the fleet is not instant.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:43.511
It's never atomic.

00:08:43.511 --> 00:08:44.885
And so, there's
an amount of time

00:08:44.885 --> 00:08:47.220
that that's going to take
that could be perceived

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.530
as sort of deploy time.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.090
Just reiterating.

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:58.150
At the point we build this
dependency resolution,

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:00.140
we have some caching
mechanisms there.

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:03.650
If you depend on a particular
version of a particular module

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:08.060
or library, and we
can reliably identify

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:11.990
that we've seen that before,
we'll pull it from cache.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:14.150
And then the rolling deploy.

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:16.070
Again, it's not atomic.

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:18.346
It happens over
the course of time,

00:09:18.346 --> 00:09:19.970
depending on how much
traffic you have.

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:21.636
If you have a lot of
traffic and there's

00:09:21.636 --> 00:09:25.100
a lot of instances
of the old version,

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:29.940
then it will take longer for
the roll out to complete.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.380
If dependencies change, a
side effect of the caching,

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.709
obviously if
dependencies change,

00:09:34.709 --> 00:09:36.250
then the build time
might take longer

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:37.375
because they're not cached.

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:45.170
OK, what happens when we serve?

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:48.520
So we have, from the diagram
before, serving and scheduling.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:52.430
These red boxes represent the
instances of your application.

00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:55.030
A request comes in.

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:58.000
If we don't have
an instance that

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:02.420
is available to serve a request,
then we'll create a new one.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:03.490
We'll scale up.

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:06.000
This implies a cold start.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.080
I might be preaching
to the converted

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.000
here in the sense of
what a cold start is,

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:16.820
but it warrants some detail.

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:18.990
OK, I'm going to
take a quick detour.

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:21.970
I put this slide in to
remind me that it's a detour.

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:24.296
And I want to talk about
three things really quickly.

00:10:24.296 --> 00:10:25.670
This is a little
bit complicated,

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:28.070
but I'll do my best.

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:31.690
Concurrency and billing.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:35.380
Products like Cloud Functions,
one of the advantages of this

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:37.660
is you're not paying
when you're not using it.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.670
And even more fine
grained than that,

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:43.390
you're only paying while
your code is executing.

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:46.130
Now, you can imagine that,
let's say I have a function

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:51.130
and I've said, I would like
512 megabytes of RAM, please.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:53.890
And my function
executes for 10 seconds,

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:57.580
and so I'm paying for 10 seconds
of wall clock time multiplied

00:10:57.580 --> 00:10:59.680
by the amount of memory
and the amount of CPU

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:03.020
that I've been granted
for that period of time.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:06.250
If I was to put another request
at the same time in there,

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:09.510
the billing gets a
little bit complicated.

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:13.040
Am I being charged for both
requests of that 512 megabyte?

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:14.330
Am I sharing it somehow?

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:17.690
What if they're not at the
same time, one starts now,

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:19.280
one starts halfway through?

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.230
It starts to get very,
very complicated.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:24.500
The simplicity of
the billing model

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:28.520
means that we are constrained to
a single request per instance.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:32.030
So a function is built
per request independent

00:11:32.030 --> 00:11:33.020
of the instance.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.540
It's not your problem whether
there's 1,000 instances or one

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:38.180
instance to serve that request.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:40.790
We might be doing a really
poor job of scheduling

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:43.570
and have 1,000 instances
sitting around costing us money,

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:45.500
but it's not costing
you anything.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:51.350
An app, conversely, you pay for
the life cycle of the instance

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.254
independent of the request.

00:11:53.254 --> 00:11:55.170
This is a fundamental
difference in the model.

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:56.670
And the reason I
have the slide here

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.520
is just to make this clear.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.040
An app will deal with
many concurrent requests

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:03.300
because it doesn't affect
the billing at all.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:04.925
The only thing that
affects the billing

00:12:04.925 --> 00:12:06.620
is the life cycle
of that instance.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:08.090
And that instance will
stay alive as long

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:09.131
as it's serving requests.

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:17.010
So this has an implication
to things like cold start.

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.020
In this chart
along the bottom, I

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:20.790
have the frequency
of cold starts.

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:23.190
How often do they occur?

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:25.740
And on the y-axis upwards,
I have the duration.

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:29.460
How long does each
cold start take?

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:33.900
These two happy faces are
places that we would be OK with.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:36.700
One where a cold
start happens a lot,

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:38.320
but it doesn't take very long.

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:41.472
And the other where it might
take a long time to start,

00:12:41.472 --> 00:12:43.055
but it doesn't happen
very frequently.

00:12:43.055 --> 00:12:46.650
A virtual machine is very
much on the top left,

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:50.610
and we hope Cloud Function is
very much on the bottom right.

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:52.710
It is necessarily,
definitionally,

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:54.960
going to have more
cold starts because it

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:58.080
deals with a single concurrent
request and with scaling

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.100
up and down.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.050
We don't want a lot
of machines sitting

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:02.160
around costing us money.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.420
That means we have
to charge you more.

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:06.840
So we're going to try to
be reactive, very quickly,

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:09.880
to these spikes and
troughs in traffic.

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:13.270
But it means that you want
the cold start time to be low.

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:18.890
Typically, this
manifests in a difference

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.110
between the primitive.

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:24.140
An app will typically cold
start less frequently.

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:25.970
And you can imagine
this scenario.

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:28.190
An app, let's say, takes
10 concurrent requests

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.420
before its resources
are exhausted

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:32.270
and we need another
instance of it.

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:35.975
If you only have 10
requests, 10 QPS, let's say,

00:13:35.975 --> 00:13:37.100
you only need one instance.

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:39.660
So that is starting
very infrequently.

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:46.240
You definitely don't
want to be this person,

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:48.680
very frequent cold starts
that take a long time.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.040
That's the worst place to be.

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:56.200
This would be the awesome place
to be, but it's a bit mythical.

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:59.680
Very, very infrequent
cold starts

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:01.512
that don't take very
much time at all.

00:14:01.512 --> 00:14:02.470
I mean, it does happen.

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:04.090
You might have a
function that has

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.010
a very quick cold
start that's cold

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:07.880
once a day and that's great.

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:10.810
But if you're building
a large application,

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:13.647
the likelihood of this
happening is pretty low.

00:14:13.647 --> 00:14:15.730
But as long as you're in
one of those green spots,

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:18.080
you're probably OK.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:20.460
OK, so this makes
the distinction,

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:22.740
the choice you're making,
between the primitive pretty

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:24.360
important.

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:27.810
If you're building something
that is multi-purpose,

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.570
so it has many endpoints--
like imagine a RESTful API

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.610
that has many end points.

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:34.020
Maybe there's some shared state.

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:36.300
Maybe you have a connection
pool, or some bit

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:39.210
of memory that you're
sharing between requests,

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:42.510
and you want to deal with many
concurrent requests at once

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:44.410
because of this shared state.

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:47.040
Then you would err on
the side of an app.

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:49.890
If, however, your compute
unit is single purpose,

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:52.320
it really just has one job,
there's no shared state,

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.140
all the state is stored
outside and you're

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:58.110
OK with a single
request at a time--

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:01.560
by the way, single request at
a time is often much simpler.

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:03.690
You have to do with far
fewer race conditions

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:05.700
when you only have a
single request in scope

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:06.930
at any on moment.

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:11.335
Then a function is where you
would tend to find yourself.

00:15:11.335 --> 00:15:12.710
OK, there's the
end of my detour.

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:21.890
Again, on this idea of
app versus function,

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:24.770
in the app case-- and this
chart represents CPU time.

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:28.100
So m cycles or clock cycles.

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:31.040
And then along the
x-axis, the wall time,

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:34.860
the time back in the real world.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:37.820
Imagine you have two
requests sequentially.

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:40.460
One request starts and
finishes, second request starts

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:44.320
and finishes, and some
amount of time in between.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:48.920
The blue line here represents
CPU usage, effectively.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:51.420
So in an app, let's
say the first request

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:54.970
uses a little bit of CPU, the
second request uses a lot.

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:56.890
And there's this
time in the middle

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:59.270
where the CPU is being used.

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:01.900
This is important
because, as I said before,

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.090
the billing model says
I'm paying for the life

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:06.100
cycle of an instance.

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:07.600
So I should be
able to do anything

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:09.700
I like during that life cycle.

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:12.880
And this is true in
the case of CPU usage.

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:19.130
If I'm only paying while
my request is in scope, as

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:23.150
in the function
case, then there's

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:26.840
not going to be any CPU activity
in between those requests

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:29.870
because there's no billing
model that accommodates for it.

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:34.160
So request number one, the
billing starts on the left.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:37.370
And then when that request
ends, we stop charging you.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:41.210
If we allowed your
CPU usage in that gap,

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:44.840
free bitcoins for everyone.

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:48.170
So this will become
important further on.

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:51.410
So an app model bills for
the lifecycle of an instance.

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:53.780
It handles concurrent
requests, and accommodates

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:56.760
for compute time outside
of the scope of request.

00:16:56.760 --> 00:17:00.020
A function bills for the
lifetime of a request,

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:02.330
handles a single
concurrent request,

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:04.760
and assumes this sort
of freeze/thaw model

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:07.470
in between requests
where no CPU activity is

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:08.720
going to be allowed to happen.

00:17:12.119 --> 00:17:13.170
OK, eviction.

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:16.319
So we talked about,
request comes in,

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:17.839
we don't have an
instance available,

00:17:17.839 --> 00:17:19.910
so we're going to create
an instance for you.

00:17:19.910 --> 00:17:21.950
And then you have a
trough in your traffic,

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:24.369
and we have 1,000
instances lying around.

00:17:24.369 --> 00:17:27.710
What policy do we use to evict
these instances, to remove them

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:30.210
from our infrastructure?

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.070
OK, that's just showing
what an eviction is.

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:36.840
So generally, instance
eviction is lazy.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:42.600
Meaning that we will tend
towards evicting instances when

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:45.360
we need the infrastructure
that they are consuming,

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:47.100
but not before.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:50.430
The reason for that is that
the machines on the data center

00:17:50.430 --> 00:17:53.790
floor largely have a fixed
cost whether your process is

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:55.620
running there or not.

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:57.630
There's an
opportunity cost to us

00:17:57.630 --> 00:18:00.030
if you have a process
that's running

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:02.640
and we need that space
for somebody else,

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:03.990
and you're not using that space.

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:07.240
In which case,
we'll kick you out.

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:09.460
Generally, though,
what we try to optimize

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:12.490
for is not whether we are
creating or destroying

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:13.180
instances.

00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:16.330
That's much, much more of
an implementation detail.

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:18.800
We just try to optimize
for performance.

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:20.320
We monitor things like latency.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:21.880
We monitor things
like cold starts

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:24.260
because they're bad
for performance.

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:28.090
And we try to optimize
the scheduling algorithms

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:30.460
or try to alter the scheduling
algorithms to optimize

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:32.470
for those two things.

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:34.620
As a side effect
of the concurrency

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:39.710
that I described
before, apps will

00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:42.960
tend to have cold
starts less frequently.

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:47.420
And that's because when each
subsequent request comes in,

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:49.330
it can be dealt with
on the same instance.

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:55.460
The number of instances we have
to create to serve the same QPS

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:57.850
is fewer.

00:18:57.850 --> 00:18:59.830
And then the last
point is, sometimes

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:01.540
instances just go bad.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:03.430
Maybe you hit an
out of memory error.

00:19:03.430 --> 00:19:05.500
And even though
that request that

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:08.380
calls the out of memory
error is long gone,

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:12.820
the machine or the instance has
found itself in a bad state.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:15.940
We'll detect that, and will
cease to send traffic to them.

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:17.890
Because we're ceasing
to send traffic to them,

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:20.350
they will become viable for
eviction at some later point.

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:25.590
OK, quick recap.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:27.670
Serverless is awesome.

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:30.870
It's also not awesome when you
can't know what's going on.

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:32.400
Google knows how to serverless.

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:33.960
You all do, too, now.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:37.260
So I expect to see many
serverless complete competitors

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:39.794
emerging after
this presentation.

00:19:39.794 --> 00:19:41.460
And then we talked
about the life cycle.

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.110
What happens at deployment?

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:46.470
We talked about atomicity of
deployment, instance creation,

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:49.122
destruction, cold starts,
apps versus functions.

00:19:49.122 --> 00:19:51.330
Background threads we didn't
talk about specifically,

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:55.057
but this was the compute
time in between the request.

00:19:55.057 --> 00:19:59.279
OK, but what do I do with all
of this amazing information

00:19:59.279 --> 00:20:00.320
that I've just been told?

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:08.830
There's some sort of
patterns and anti-patterns

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:10.850
that I want to talk about.

00:20:10.850 --> 00:20:15.970
So let's look at some common
pitfalls that people fall into.

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:20.420
Number one, are we choosing
the right primitive?

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:24.290
Are we trying to jam
an entire Java Spring

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:25.634
application into a function?

00:20:25.634 --> 00:20:27.050
And is that the
right thing to do?

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:34.200
What can we do to improve
our cold start time?

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:35.640
What are the best
practices there?

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:38.027
Oops, I'm sorry.

00:20:38.027 --> 00:20:39.610
What do we do with
background threads?

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:41.776
I have this thread that's
running in the background.

00:20:41.776 --> 00:20:43.720
A common pattern
in web applications

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:46.780
is receive the request,
respond quickly, and do

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:48.250
some work in the background.

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.290
Well, that's not going
to work in a function

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:52.990
because once you've
sent the response back,

00:20:52.990 --> 00:20:54.730
that's a signal for
us that you're done.

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:56.438
And we're going to
throttle the CPU down,

00:20:56.438 --> 00:21:00.029
and your background task
may or may not complete.

00:21:00.029 --> 00:21:01.570
What do you do with
this auto scaling

00:21:01.570 --> 00:21:05.427
where we scale up beautifully
and you denial of service

00:21:05.427 --> 00:21:07.760
your own database because it
can't deal with the traffic

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:10.400
that we've been sending it
because we auto scale up?

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:12.550
And then I'll talk a little
bit about idempotency

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:13.216
towards the end.

00:21:17.630 --> 00:21:21.780
Number one, choose
the right primitive.

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:25.260
Do you want to put your big Java
monolith into a Cloud Function?

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:27.030
Probably not.

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:27.669
You can.

00:21:27.669 --> 00:21:29.710
You're going to have
horrendous cold start times,

00:21:29.710 --> 00:21:31.620
and they're going to
happen very frequently,

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:35.300
and everyone has a bad time.

00:21:35.300 --> 00:21:39.500
Do I put my big Java
monolith into App Engine?

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:41.100
Maybe.

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:41.970
Why wouldn't I?

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:46.600
Well, I wouldn't if I'm
relying a lot on state.

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:49.390
Serverless compute
products work best

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:52.430
when they assume that
they're stateless.

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:56.060
If you have to
persist state, you

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:59.390
will find circumstances
where the request where

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:02.860
you assume that state was there
finds that it's not there.

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:05.016
Now, it's a great
performance optimization

00:22:05.016 --> 00:22:06.390
that you might be
caching things.

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:07.940
And if it's there, it works
faster, and if it's not,

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:09.180
you can go get it.

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:13.030
And that's fine, but
this is why I say maybe.

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:15.340
The best thing to
do is to make sure

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:19.870
that the application has
the appropriate granularity

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:21.580
of services.

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:23.530
Very, very tiny services,
Cloud functions.

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:26.537
Medium sized
services, App Engine.

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:34.480
Some anti-patterns with
relation to cold starts.

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:39.180
Again, if you assume a
stateful environment,

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:41.940
then this might be problematic.

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:46.810
What I mean by that is that in
a classic monolithic web app,

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:50.760
it's very common for the start
to load a bunch of state.

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:54.900
Maybe it's loading things
from a database into cache.

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:57.640
Maybe it's instantiating
objects ahead of time,

00:22:57.640 --> 00:23:00.900
instantiating singletons,
whatever the case may be.

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:03.270
This design pattern
assumes, essentially,

00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:05.190
the virtual machine
style of mode

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:07.670
of operating where
you start up the VM,

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:08.670
it does a bunch of work.

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:10.150
It might take five
minutes to start,

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:11.525
but that's OK
because you're only

00:23:11.525 --> 00:23:14.220
doing it like once a month.

00:23:14.220 --> 00:23:16.700
This doesn't translate
well to serverless

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:19.460
because your start/stop
cadence is much more frequent.

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:26.550
And then the last point there
is this primitive choice.

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:28.760
Choose the right primitive.

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:31.580
Now, we at Google need
to do the right job

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:35.000
in making that easy for you.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:38.550
But really, choosing
the right primitives

00:23:38.550 --> 00:23:41.566
set you off on the right
foot from the beginning.

00:23:44.542 --> 00:23:50.470
OK, here's some code, in
case you didn't notice.

00:23:50.470 --> 00:23:54.110
If there's any Node.js
developers in the audience,

00:23:54.110 --> 00:23:55.660
don't come and
attack me afterwards

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:58.060
because what I'm going
to show you is pretty

00:23:58.060 --> 00:24:00.610
much the antithesis of what
a Node.js developer will

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:02.980
tell you is best practice.

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:04.774
And they'd be
right, but I'm going

00:24:04.774 --> 00:24:06.190
to show it to you
anyway because I

00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:08.230
think it's a good illustration.

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:12.410
This would be pretty common
in a Node application.

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:15.320
And I'll just unpack
what's happening here.

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:17.450
I have some libraries,
some dependencies.

00:24:17.450 --> 00:24:21.170
They are being required at the
top of my file, the first thing

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:22.670
that happens.

00:24:22.670 --> 00:24:25.850
And then I have two functions
declared in the same JavaScript

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:26.480
file.

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:28.820
I have do something,
and do something else.

00:24:28.820 --> 00:24:31.970
Each function uses only
one of those dependencies.

00:24:31.970 --> 00:24:36.380
And I said this would be totally
sane pattern in an application

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:38.880
model or on a virtual machine.

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:40.882
The first function uses
the first dependency.

00:24:40.882 --> 00:24:42.840
The second function uses
the second dependency.

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:44.486
Everyone's happy.

00:24:44.486 --> 00:24:47.390
But the problem is, the
second function doesn't

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:48.470
use the first dependency.

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:51.500
So if I was deploying these
as separate Cloud Functions

00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:54.590
even though they're in
the same source file,

00:24:54.590 --> 00:24:57.120
the do something else
would be its own function.

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:00.000
But at cold start time, it would
be loading the first library

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.020
even though it
never, ever uses it.

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:05.470
Now, multiply this by 10.

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:07.330
How do you know that
all of the things

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:09.280
that you're doing at the
start-- and I'm doing

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:10.571
something pretty innocent here.

00:25:10.571 --> 00:25:12.550
All I'm doing is following
Node best practice.

00:25:12.550 --> 00:25:13.716
I'm just requiring a bunch--

00:25:13.716 --> 00:25:16.360
I'm not loading a cache, and
I'm not doing anything silly.

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:18.790
I followed what they told me.

00:25:18.790 --> 00:25:23.150
But the side effect of this
is that each function, when

00:25:23.150 --> 00:25:25.790
deployed individually, is
bearing the cost of all

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:29.190
the other functions.

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:30.949
So this is unwanted
cold start time.

00:25:30.949 --> 00:25:33.240
This doesn't offer me anything,
it just costs me money.

00:25:36.936 --> 00:25:40.130
Here's a more insidious example.

00:25:40.130 --> 00:25:42.810
The function is
using both libraries,

00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:45.860
but only conditionally.

00:25:45.860 --> 00:25:50.690
So if my query string foo
only equals bar in one

00:25:50.690 --> 00:25:55.490
out of a million requests,
then all the other requests

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:59.300
are paying the price for that
in terms of cold start time.

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:03.270
This takes a little bit of
relearning in how we do things.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:06.380
We have to be mindful of what's
going to happen at startup

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:08.460
when I deploy this thing.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:11.120
We often hear, oh,
such-and-such product

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:15.920
or such-and-such platform has
good or bad code start times.

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:20.647
But largely, it's dependent
on this code that we write.

00:26:20.647 --> 00:26:22.730
OK, this is where I'm going
to get in big trouble.

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:30.840
I'm not saying necessarily
do this, but this would help.

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:39.380
I only require the library
when it's actually required.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:40.890
Now, a really great
feature of Node

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:44.100
is that that require
statement will be cached.

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:46.440
And so, the
subsequent invocation

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:51.570
of that on a hot instance,
it's effectively a no-op.

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:53.490
But this is, again,
just illustrative.

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:58.980
The ELSE condition that happens
in most cases, let's say,

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:02.820
doesn't pay the cost
of some huge lib that's

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:05.841
loaded in the IF condition.

00:27:05.841 --> 00:27:08.510
Hope that makes sense.

00:27:08.510 --> 00:27:10.712
OK, I want to invite my
colleague Monika on stage,

00:27:10.712 --> 00:27:12.170
and she's going to
give you a demo.

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:18.637
Can we switch to demo, please?

00:27:18.637 --> 00:27:19.970
MONIKA NAWROT: Thank you, Jason.

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:21.740
So my name is Monika Nawrot.

00:27:21.740 --> 00:27:24.770
I'm a software engineer
in Cloud Functions team.

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:26.600
And for the purpose
of this talk,

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:29.570
I created a demo
that illustrates

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:30.980
what Jason has just said.

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:38.100
So it operates on cold start
and lifecycle of Cloud Function.

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:42.680
So in my demo, I
created three functions

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:45.830
that do more or
less the same thing,

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:48.840
but in a slightly different way.

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:52.430
So I integrated
functions with Slack.

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:54.840
So Slack is this
cloud-based tool

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:58.580
that aids team collaboration.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:00.170
So I have my Slack channel.

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:05.940
And I also created a
free custom command that

00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:08.520
are hard coded with functions.

00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:10.250
So integration is very easy.

00:28:10.250 --> 00:28:12.770
As you can see,
there is a command

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:14.880
and there's a URL
that should be called

00:28:14.880 --> 00:28:18.170
whenever this command is called,
and it points to a function.

00:28:20.790 --> 00:28:23.465
So let's try our first function.

00:28:30.530 --> 00:28:36.770
So all of these functions do the
same thing, as I said before.

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:40.320
So they expect a term
in the Slack channel,

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:43.370
and they put this term
into Knowledge Graph API

00:28:43.370 --> 00:28:46.095
and [INAUDIBLE]
information about this term

00:28:46.095 --> 00:28:46.970
from Knowledge Graph.

00:28:46.970 --> 00:28:51.140
And then it returns a response
which contains the data

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:52.460
from the Knowledge Graph.

00:28:52.460 --> 00:28:55.640
So we can learn something
about this particular thing

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:57.260
from Knowledge Graph API.

00:28:57.260 --> 00:29:01.280
So the first function is written
in application like style.

00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:06.120
So as you can see, first
we extract the text.

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:10.050
So the term that was written
in the Slack channel.

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:11.810
Then we used a local cache.

00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:15.410
So first, we tried to retrieve
information from the cache.

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:20.300
And if it's not there, then we
are making the actual query.

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:24.860
The query is on here, so we just
put the query to the Knowledge

00:29:24.860 --> 00:29:29.060
Graph API, and we
extract the first result.

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:31.420
And then we do the formatting.

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:35.360
So we are converting the
message returned from the API

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:40.370
to the format that can be
displayed on the Slack channel.

00:29:40.370 --> 00:29:43.740
So because it's application
like style function,

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:45.830
I also do some preloading.

00:29:45.830 --> 00:29:49.190
So let's say that my Slack
channel is about traveling,

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:53.150
and people often look for
information on the countries.

00:29:53.150 --> 00:29:55.910
So I want to make this
experience for them

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:58.050
really good, really fast.

00:29:58.050 --> 00:30:01.910
So I'm preloading some data
associated with countries.

00:30:01.910 --> 00:30:06.120
So in another file, I have
the list of all countries.

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:08.990
So now let me go
to my Slack channel

00:30:08.990 --> 00:30:16.610
and try this, try to
call the first function.

00:30:16.610 --> 00:30:19.660
And what actually
happened is that Slack

00:30:19.660 --> 00:30:27.020
has quite an aggressive timeout
for the responses from the web

00:30:27.020 --> 00:30:29.760
[INAUDIBLE] for the comments.

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:33.140
And because we are
doing this preloading,

00:30:33.140 --> 00:30:37.340
we experience the situation
when actually the function was

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:40.430
executed correctly, but
our Slack didn't wait

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:48.086
for the result. However, like
any further crawl for the API,

00:30:48.086 --> 00:30:52.650
it should be extremely,
extremely quick.

00:30:52.650 --> 00:30:55.610
So now let me go to the
logs of this function

00:30:55.610 --> 00:31:03.810
just to see how much did it take
for the function to execute.

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:07.490
So as you can see,
the first integration

00:31:07.490 --> 00:31:09.770
took more than three
seconds just because we're

00:31:09.770 --> 00:31:11.600
preloading all the terms.

00:31:11.600 --> 00:31:16.610
However, any consecutive request
was processed very quickly

00:31:16.610 --> 00:31:19.520
because we could serve
the response directly

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:22.080
from the cache.

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:26.910
So now let's go to
the second function,

00:31:26.910 --> 00:31:31.400
which would be written in a way
that it's completely stateless.

00:31:31.400 --> 00:31:35.940
So this is the kind of thing
that you can expect when

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:38.990
the writing code functions.

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:44.230
So again, I'm extracting
a text from the request.

00:31:44.230 --> 00:31:46.600
Then I'm not using a
local cache as just

00:31:46.600 --> 00:31:49.975
before, I'm just directly
calling Knowledge Graph

00:31:49.975 --> 00:31:53.986
API each time I'm
calling this function.

00:31:53.986 --> 00:31:58.330
I have also this
function.form as a query,

00:31:58.330 --> 00:32:02.230
and I do not do any preloading.

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:04.690
So now let's go
back to the channel

00:32:04.690 --> 00:32:06.180
and try the second function.

00:32:09.050 --> 00:32:19.250
So as you can see, I'm receiving
the results reasonably quickly.

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:22.310
Maybe try one more time.

00:32:22.310 --> 00:32:25.530
So in general, we
can see it works.

00:32:25.530 --> 00:32:28.760
So let's, again, look
at the exact numbers,

00:32:28.760 --> 00:32:34.140
how long this function
was executed each time.

00:32:34.140 --> 00:32:38.790
So as you can see, each
time it takes a few hundred

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:42.150
milliseconds to finish.

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:47.480
However, the user experience
is definitely better,

00:32:47.480 --> 00:32:50.820
but still the times
are not perfect.

00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:53.840
So really, the
best approach would

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:58.400
be the one presented
in the first function.

00:32:58.400 --> 00:33:01.430
So we can go to the source.

00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:04.580
So again, we are
extracting the query,

00:33:04.580 --> 00:33:07.270
but we also used a local cache.

00:33:07.270 --> 00:33:12.440
And so, we're trying to retrieve
the result from local cache

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:13.430
if it's there.

00:33:13.430 --> 00:33:17.030
If it's not, we are querying
Knowledge Graph API.

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:18.770
However, we don't
use any preloading.

00:33:18.770 --> 00:33:21.020
So we optimized
for a cold start,

00:33:21.020 --> 00:33:25.580
but we still think
that it makes sense

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:27.545
to use cache on best-for basis.

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:35.035
So let's see how it works.

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:46.600
So for different
terms, it works similar

00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:48.380
to the previous function.

00:33:48.380 --> 00:33:51.970
However, for the terms that I'm
executing for the second time,

00:33:51.970 --> 00:33:55.210
the function executes
much quicker because they

00:33:55.210 --> 00:33:58.660
could be served from the cache.

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:00.760
So let me see the logs.

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:04.450
So as you can see,
there's still a cold

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:07.030
that takes longer than
consecutive integration

00:34:07.030 --> 00:34:10.550
because this new instance
needed to be created.

00:34:10.550 --> 00:34:14.889
However, any consecutive
requests for the same term

00:34:14.889 --> 00:34:17.330
can be very quick.

00:34:17.330 --> 00:34:21.020
So to summarize, this
demo shows two things.

00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:23.870
The first, that you should
truly optimize for a cold

00:34:23.870 --> 00:34:25.900
start because they happen.

00:34:25.900 --> 00:34:28.270
They happen any time
traffic pattern changes,

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:33.340
and new function instance
needs to be created.

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:36.520
And like as you can
see in this example,

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:41.560
this cold start time,
if it's too long,

00:34:41.560 --> 00:34:46.120
may mean that your function
is unavailable for the user.

00:34:46.120 --> 00:34:49.840
And the second thing, that
the platform is smart enough

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:53.980
so we will not dispose your
function instance just after it

00:34:53.980 --> 00:34:56.980
was executed because it
can anticipate that there

00:34:56.980 --> 00:34:58.400
will be some traffic.

00:34:58.400 --> 00:35:01.810
So it still makes sense
to use a local cache

00:35:01.810 --> 00:35:04.550
to optimize the results.

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:07.690
So this is all that I
prepared, so we can switch back

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:09.200
to the presentation.

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:10.450
JASON POLITES: Thanks, Monika.

00:35:15.810 --> 00:35:18.900
Yeah, so what we just saw
there was a much better version

00:35:18.900 --> 00:35:24.650
of what I had put on screen
actually done by real engineer.

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:28.440
OK, so just to summarize.

00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:31.340
It's important to understand
and optimize for cold starts.

00:35:31.340 --> 00:35:33.990
They happen when a new
instance is created.

00:35:33.990 --> 00:35:36.600
We will create new instances
when they're needed.

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:39.800
And then, because functions only
serve a single current request

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:43.290
because billing, that
happens even more frequently

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:44.320
in the functions case.

00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:50.210
Again, this period of
time in between requests

00:35:50.210 --> 00:35:51.590
in the function
case, what do you

00:35:51.590 --> 00:35:57.077
do with background threads
or background tasks

00:35:57.077 --> 00:35:58.160
that you might be running?

00:35:58.160 --> 00:36:02.060
As I said, it's very common
in the sort of web application

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:05.454
world, that you might want to
early respond to a request,

00:36:05.454 --> 00:36:07.370
and then do some work,
some more heavy lifting

00:36:07.370 --> 00:36:09.290
in the background.

00:36:09.290 --> 00:36:11.180
This is not going
to work very well

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:14.300
in a function abstraction
because of this CPU throttling

00:36:14.300 --> 00:36:16.590
behavior.

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:19.940
The best practice here is, if
possible, wait for all the work

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:22.340
to complete before you return.

00:36:22.340 --> 00:36:27.740
If you want to give the user
or the caller a response early,

00:36:27.740 --> 00:36:32.900
then we would recommend that
you use some sort of event bus,

00:36:32.900 --> 00:36:36.796
like a Pub/Sub, and queue a
message on that event bus.

00:36:36.796 --> 00:36:38.420
Have some other
function somewhere else

00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:40.400
that's going to do that
background processing,

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:41.810
and then return.

00:36:41.810 --> 00:36:45.650
And it's not just because of
this CPU throttling behavior

00:36:45.650 --> 00:36:47.060
that we would say this.

00:36:47.060 --> 00:36:50.365
You can imagine the request
comes in, I spawn a thread,

00:36:50.365 --> 00:36:52.240
I'm going to do some
heavy lifting over here,

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:53.670
but I'm responding immediately.

00:36:53.670 --> 00:36:55.670
And then I get halfway
through the heavy lifting

00:36:55.670 --> 00:36:57.470
and the thing crashes
for whatever reason.

00:36:57.470 --> 00:36:59.810
Out of memory, process dies.

00:36:59.810 --> 00:37:00.960
What happened to that work?

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:03.530
What are the guarantees
that I'm providing myself

00:37:03.530 --> 00:37:04.580
over that work?

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:09.200
Probably much better is to have
and at least once guarantee

00:37:09.200 --> 00:37:12.980
on an event pass like Pub/Sub
so that if it does fail,

00:37:12.980 --> 00:37:14.870
it will retry and
eventually succeed.

00:37:20.100 --> 00:37:22.260
Special call out to
connection pools,

00:37:22.260 --> 00:37:25.230
somewhat unsolved problem.

00:37:25.230 --> 00:37:29.850
Traditional database, MySQL,
Postgres, take your pick,

00:37:29.850 --> 00:37:32.340
will have a finite
number of connections

00:37:32.340 --> 00:37:36.630
it can deal with on a particular
instance or in aggregate.

00:37:36.630 --> 00:37:39.040
You have a spike in traffic.

00:37:39.040 --> 00:37:42.990
It overwhelms the database,
too many connections.

00:37:42.990 --> 00:37:47.910
And this is displayed
here in my nice graphic.

00:37:51.080 --> 00:37:54.180
There's a couple of
things you can do here.

00:37:54.180 --> 00:37:56.070
There isn't really a
silver bullet for this

00:37:56.070 --> 00:37:57.600
because it's a
fundamental mismatch.

00:37:57.600 --> 00:37:59.970
There's a fundamental
sort of impedance mismatch

00:37:59.970 --> 00:38:04.830
between a finitely scaled
resource and a, I hesitate

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:09.000
to say infinitely, but much
larger scaled compute layer

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:10.260
above that.

00:38:10.260 --> 00:38:15.750
Couple of things you can do,
data access service pattern

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:17.520
and scaling controls.

00:38:17.520 --> 00:38:21.000
I think I repeat myself there.

00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:23.989
So limit exposure to the
database to only the services

00:38:23.989 --> 00:38:24.530
that need it.

00:38:24.530 --> 00:38:27.196
Don't just throw your connection
pool into every single function

00:38:27.196 --> 00:38:27.720
you do.

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:32.930
Be diligent and judicious about
who is accessing the database.

00:38:32.930 --> 00:38:36.300
One other idea is to break
your database up itself.

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:39.240
Now, it's sometimes difficult if
you're relying on foreign keys

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:40.360
and things like that.

00:38:40.360 --> 00:38:43.410
But as a pattern,
it can be useful.

00:38:43.410 --> 00:38:46.320
Limit connection pools,
the connection pool size.

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:50.220
And we have this somewhat
strange recommendation

00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:53.070
that we make in Cloud Functions
where you create a connection

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:55.380
pool to a database like
MySQL, and you limit it

00:38:55.380 --> 00:38:56.730
to a size of one.

00:38:56.730 --> 00:38:58.710
Why do I need a pool
if it's a pool of one?

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:00.390
Why don't I just
make a connection?

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:04.342
Well, it's limited to one
because, first of all,

00:39:04.342 --> 00:39:06.550
you're only dealing with a
single concurrent request.

00:39:06.550 --> 00:39:08.674
So the likelihood of you
ever needing more than one

00:39:08.674 --> 00:39:10.770
is pretty low.

00:39:10.770 --> 00:39:12.730
And secondly, if you
just left it the default,

00:39:12.730 --> 00:39:14.380
and let's say the connection
pool framework you're

00:39:14.380 --> 00:39:17.047
using says, I'm going to create
10 connections by default,

00:39:17.047 --> 00:39:18.630
every single instance
of that function

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:21.300
is going to create 10
database connections.

00:39:21.300 --> 00:39:23.640
You're going to run out of
connections pretty quickly.

00:39:23.640 --> 00:39:25.650
The reason we say to
use a connection pool

00:39:25.650 --> 00:39:28.710
is really just a convenience
because many connection pool

00:39:28.710 --> 00:39:31.590
frameworks will do things like
reestablish the connection

00:39:31.590 --> 00:39:33.310
if it's found to be bad.

00:39:33.310 --> 00:39:35.070
And so, you just get
greater reliability

00:39:35.070 --> 00:39:37.230
by leveraging that
kind of retry behavior

00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:40.360
that's native to
the connection pool.

00:39:40.360 --> 00:39:44.650
Another thing you can do is use
rate limits at an API level,

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:47.035
or scaling limits at the
infrastructure level.

00:39:47.035 --> 00:39:48.910
For those who saw the
presentation yesterday,

00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:51.010
we talked about a new
feature in Cloud Functions

00:39:51.010 --> 00:39:54.100
that's coming soon which allows
you to limit the amount that we

00:39:54.100 --> 00:39:54.940
scale up.

00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:56.590
So you can have
some levers there

00:39:56.590 --> 00:39:59.860
to say, please don't
DDoS my database.

00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:03.910
I can only deal with
100 connections.

00:40:03.910 --> 00:40:06.250
I know I have one
connection per instance.

00:40:06.250 --> 00:40:10.060
I know I have 100
instances maximum.

00:40:10.060 --> 00:40:13.750
Or use a, quote unquote,
serverless database

00:40:13.750 --> 00:40:16.820
like Cloud Datastore where we
handle that scaling for you.

00:40:19.770 --> 00:40:25.460
OK, last point, idempotency.

00:40:25.460 --> 00:40:30.440
I have a quote there, which
is sort of often attributed

00:40:30.440 --> 00:40:31.130
to Einstein.

00:40:31.130 --> 00:40:33.650
I'm not sure if he
actually said it.

00:40:33.650 --> 00:40:35.360
Doing the same thing
over and over again

00:40:35.360 --> 00:40:37.640
and expecting different results.

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:39.170
Was he talking
about idempotency?

00:40:39.170 --> 00:40:41.150
I'm not sure.

00:40:41.150 --> 00:40:45.620
So idempotency is when
I perform some operation

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:48.276
and no matter how many times
I perform that operation,

00:40:48.276 --> 00:40:49.400
the outcome doesn't change.

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:54.210
It changes the first time, and
then doesn't change thereafter.

00:40:54.210 --> 00:40:57.780
So this is really important
because in many circumstances,

00:40:57.780 --> 00:41:00.410
particularly in asynchronous
event-based functions,

00:41:00.410 --> 00:41:02.870
the guarantees
are at least once.

00:41:02.870 --> 00:41:05.570
At least once means will
execute you at least once,

00:41:05.570 --> 00:41:07.130
but not exactly once.

00:41:07.130 --> 00:41:10.610
And there are
circumstances where you'll

00:41:10.610 --> 00:41:11.810
be executed more than once.

00:41:14.390 --> 00:41:18.350
And I have a
reference to something

00:41:18.350 --> 00:41:20.550
like an UPSERT there where
you can mutate the data.

00:41:20.550 --> 00:41:22.550
But if you do it
again, nothing changes.

00:41:29.100 --> 00:41:31.390
This is a concrete example.

00:41:31.390 --> 00:41:35.580
This is actually my inbox from
about two or three weeks ago.

00:41:35.580 --> 00:41:37.980
I don't know if this was
an idempotency thing,

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:40.890
but it seems like
it may have been.

00:41:40.890 --> 00:41:43.340
And actually, it
was more than 19.

00:41:43.340 --> 00:41:46.360
I took the screenshot
too prematurely.

00:41:46.360 --> 00:41:49.120
But you can imagine a situation
where you have a bit of code

00:41:49.120 --> 00:41:51.200
and it's sending an email.

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:54.220
But it's sending an email, and
then it's doing something else.

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:56.811
And the something else fails
and they have a retry policy,

00:41:56.811 --> 00:41:59.310
and so it's sending email and
then failing, and then sending

00:41:59.310 --> 00:42:00.760
an email and failing.

00:42:00.760 --> 00:42:03.110
So it is not idempotent.

00:42:03.110 --> 00:42:05.530
So a failure that leads to
a retry is correct behavior.

00:42:05.530 --> 00:42:07.440
You want this thing to happen.

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:11.650
But a failure that causes
mutations or sends SMS messages

00:42:11.650 --> 00:42:13.720
or whatever it might
be and then fails

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:16.610
is definitionally
not idempotent.

00:42:16.610 --> 00:42:19.120
So there's many ways
to solve for this.

00:42:19.120 --> 00:42:21.220
We're not going to
dig deep on that now.

00:42:21.220 --> 00:42:23.810
Just something to be mindful
of, particularly in serverless,

00:42:23.810 --> 00:42:27.100
particularly in asynchronous,
where at least once guarantees

00:42:27.100 --> 00:42:28.220
are the norm.

00:42:28.220 --> 00:42:33.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

